#N canvas 40 60 1150 760 12;
#X text 20 10 [nn.dense] - a fully connected LAYER of neurons \, working on numbers stored in arrays, f 120;
#X text 20 31 out[j] = f( b[j] + sum over i of W[j * n_in + i] * in[i] ) for every output j. This is the workhorse of modern networks: an autoencoder \, a GAN generator or a transformer is mostly stacks of dense layers., f 120;
#X text 20 67 ARGUMENTS: 1) input array 2) output array 3) activation (default tanh \, see [nn.act]). Weights live in the array <output>_w (n_out x n_in numbers \, row by row) \, biases in <output>_b (n_out numbers). Sizes are read from the arrays. Use array names without "-": they are read inside [expr]., f 120;
#X text 20 118 LEFT INLET: bang = compute \, a function name = change the activation. OUTLET: bang when the output array is ready - connect it to the next layer., f 120;
#X obj 20 164 inlet;
#X obj 20 190 route bang;
#X obj 560 494 nn.act \$3;
